\n            for neighbor, weight in graph[current_node].items():\n                new_dist = current_dist + weight\n                if neighbor not in min_dist or new_dist < min_dist[neighbor]:\n                    min_dist[neighbor] = new_dist\n                    previous[neighbor] = current_node\n                    heapq.heappush(pq, (new_dist, neighbor))\n    \n    # If end is not reachable\n    if end not in visited:\n        return (None, None)\n    \n    # Reconstruct path from end to start\n    path = []\n    current = end\n    while current is not None:\n        path.append(current)\n        current = previous.get(current)\n    \n    path.reverse()\n    \n    return (min_dist[end], path)\n```\n\nI'm reconsidering the visited set approach. In Dijkstra's algorithm, we don't need to mark nodes as permanently visited - we just skip if we've already found a shorter path to them. Let me refine this:\n\nActually, I think my current implementation is okay, but I should simplify it. The key insight is that when we pop from the heap, if we've already found a better path to that node, we skip it.\n\nLet me write a cleaner version:\n\n```python\ndef shortest_path(edges, start, end):\n    # Check for negative weights\n    for _, _, weight in edges:\n        if weight < 0:\n            raise ValueError(\"Negative weight detected\")\n    \n    # Handle same start and end\n    if start == end:\n        return (0, [start])\n    \n    # Build adjacency list with weights\n    graph = {}\n    for from_node, to_node, weight in edges:\n        if from_node not in graph:\n            graph[from_node] = {}\n        graph[from_node][to_node] = weight\n    \n    # Dijkstra's algorithm\n    import heapq\n    \n    # Distance to each node (infinity means unreachable)\n    distances = {node: float('inf') for node in set(from_node for from_node, _, _ in edges)}\n    \n    # But wait, nodes might only appear as destinations too\n    all_nodes = set()\n    for from_node, to_node, _ in 